{
  "teamId": 109,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Arizona Diamondbacks opened the year with a jolt of bad news on Opening Day, as Lourdes Gurriel Jr. torched them in the very first game of the season. But Arizona answered quickly, with Eugenio Suárez delivering a thunderous grand slam on April 1st that set the tone for what he would become: the team's most reliable power source. Through April and into May, the Diamondbacks played scrappy, competitive baseball, trading big moments with opponents in a series of tight games that left them hovering near .500 and searching for an identity.\n\nThe summer months told the story of a team perpetually one step forward and one step back. Suárez continued to mash, hitting grand slams and clutch doubles throughout, while Corbin Carroll flashed the brilliance that made him a star. But the pitching staff — particularly Shelby Miller, who was victimized repeatedly in June — couldn't hold leads when it mattered most. Costly home runs surrendered by the bullpen repeatedly erased hard-earned advantages, and a brutal stretch in mid-June, including a devastating Geraldo Perdomo triple that backfired in context, encapsulated the team's maddening inconsistency.\n\nAugust became the season's emotional peak and valley in rapid succession. Ketel Marte authored the most electric back-to-back nights of the year, hitting walk-off-caliber blasts on August 12th and 13th, with his three-run homer on the 13th registering as the single highest-leverage positive moment of the entire season. For a fleeting moment, it felt like Arizona might actually make a run. But the bullpen continued to leak, the lineup sputtered in key spots, and by late August the Diamondbacks were drowning in a sea of mediocrity they could never fully escape.\n\nThe final month of the season was a denouement of disappointment. September brought a few flickers — Gabriel Moreno's three-run homer in mid-month sparked hope — but losses kept piling up, including a season-ending gut punch courtesy of Fernando Tatis Jr.'s grand slam off Zac Gallen in the penultimate week. Arizona finished 80-82, two games below .500, a team with genuine offensive firepower — 214 home runs and nearly 800 runs scored — but not enough pitching consistency to translate talent into October baseball. The 2023 World Series run felt very far away.",
  "chapters": [
